.Dd $Mdocdate: August 5 2026 $
.Dt OPENBAR 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openbar
.Nd lightweight status bar for cwm and other X11 window managers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 1
.Op Fl c Ar path
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a status bar designed for
.Xr cwm 1
and other X11 window managers.
It displays system information including CPU speed and temperature,
free memory, load average, battery status, public and private
IP addresses, and VPN connection status.
.Pp
Its visual style, colour palette and configuration syntax are
modelled after
.Xr cwm 1
so that the bar blends in as if it were part of the window manager.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 1
Run one update cycle and exit.
.It Fl c Ar path
Use the configuration file at
.Ar path
instead of the default.
.El
.Pp
Configuration is loaded from, in order:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the file passed with
.Fl c
.It
.Pa ~/.openbarrc
if it exists
.It
.Pa /etc/openbarrc
.El
.Sh PROCESS ARCHITECTURE
.Nm
runs as one or two processes on
.Ox :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Main process
Reads configuration, manages the X11 window, and queries
kernel interfaces for CPU, memory, load, battery, and
network interface statistics.
.It Network worker
When public network information is enabled, a child process is forked
before sandboxing.
It fetches public IPv4 and IPv6 addresses from
.Pa ifconfig.me
over HTTP and returns validated results to the main
process through a bounded
.Xr socketpair 2
protocol.
The worker has no access to X11, the configuration file,
battery or sensor devices, or any other parent resource.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.openbarrc
Per-user configuration.
.It Pa /etc/openbarrc
System-wide configuration.
.El
.Sh SECURITY
.Ss Sandboxing
The network worker and, except as noted below, the display process use
.Xr pledge 2
and
.Xr unveil 2
on
.Ox
to limit permitted system calls and filesystem visibility.
.Ss Main process
Filesystem visibility is locked to:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Pa /tmp/.X11-unix
.Pq read-write, X11 socket
.It
.Ev XAUTHORITY
or
.Pa ~/.Xauthority
.Pq read-only, when the file exists
.It
.Pa /dev/apm
.Pq read-only, only if battery display is enabled and the node exists
.El
.Pp
The steady-state
.Xr pledge 2
set is derived from the enabled configuration options:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dq stdio unix
Used for basic I/O and the already-open X11 connection.
.It Dq vminfo
Added when
.Ar mem
is enabled because
.Dv VM_UVMEXP
requires it.
.It Dq route
Added when
.Ar net
or
.Ar vpn
is enabled because
.Xr getifaddrs 3
uses
.Dv NET_RT_IFLIST .
.El
.Pp
No pledge promise permits repeated
.Dv APM_IOC_GETPOWER
operations.
When
.Ar bat
is enabled, the display process therefore remains unpledged and prints a
warning; its unveil policy is still locked.
.Ss Network worker
The worker runs under
.Dq stdio inet dns
with filesystem visibility locked to
.Pa /etc/hosts ,
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf ,
.Pa /etc/services ,
and
.Pa /etc/protocols
.Pq all read-only .
.Ss IPC protocol
Communication between parent and child uses a fixed-size
message structure over
.Xr socketpair 2 .
The parent sends a one-byte fetch command; the child
responds with a bounded 112-byte response containing
status codes and the address strings.
.Ss Descriptor isolation
The child closes its standard input, standard output, and
the parent's socketpair endpoint immediately after
.Xr fork 2 .
The parent closes the child's socketpair endpoint.
No file descriptors cross the
.Xr exec 3
boundary
.Pq the child never calls exec .
.Ss Privilege dropping
.Nm
runs as the invoking user; no setuid, privilege escalation,
or
.Xr chroot 2
is performed.
.Ss Limitations
Public addresses are obtained over unencrypted HTTP and are informational.
The response is bounded and parsed with
.Xr inet_pton 3
before display.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cwm 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr apm 4 ,
.Xr openbarrc 5 ,
.Xr X 7
